// glyph-9 — round 3, and the round where it works. Two things landed between round 2 and this one.
//
// MARUTI, on round 1: "I like I, J a bit — those look closer to how a 9 feels, but it can be made
// better in terms of balance and visual aesthetic. C, D are ok but something is missing." What he
// picked out was one path segment: C and D ended the stem straight down, I and J turned. The turn
// is what makes a 9 rather than a q, and round 2 spent 25 drawings finding out how far the tail
// should turn, how far left it should reach and where it should end. Its answer was that the
// terminal belongs 1.0 to 1.5 left of the BOWL'S OWN CENTRE.
//
// THE REFERENCE SURVEY, on how thirteen sets actually draw a 9: they agree with that goal and
// differ on the means. Every set that succeeds runs a STRAIGHT DIAGONAL tail — Nucleo 29.9 degrees
// off vertical with 33 per cent of the letter projecting below the bowl, Remix 30.0 / 29 per cent,
// Font Awesome 6 36.6 / 24 per cent — leaving the bowl between 3 and 4:30 o'clock and finishing
// under it. Not one of round 1's or round 2's 50 drawings contained a straight leaning tail. That
// is the gap this board fills, and the straight and the curved are both on it so the comparison is
// Maruti's to make, not an assertion.
//
// TWO OF ROUND 1'S CONCLUSIONS ARE WITHDRAWN, and both for the same reason — they were measuring
// the vertical stem, not the 9.
//   · "It reads as a q above a 72 per cent bowl" was never about bowl share. Rows A-G of round 1
//     had a VERTICAL stem, which is a q by construction. Font Awesome sits at 76 per cent and Remix
//     at 71, both above that line, and both read as 9s. S and T here are our 76 per cent bowl with
//     a leaning tail, on the board so that can be seen rather than argued.
//   · "The counter reads mid grey at 16, therefore the letter is dead" was the wrong measure.
//     Field counters run 18-28 per cent of the letter's height; ours at a 67 per cent bowl is 19,
//     inside it. Scaled to 5.25 of ink the Nucleo, Remix and Font Awesome 9s all collapse to a dark
//     blob with a pinhole counter too. They read as 9s because of the tail. The counter number is
//     still reported for every candidate, but as a fact about the drawing, not as a verdict.
//   Our stroke is not the problem either: the field runs 0.18-0.27 stroke-to-height and ours is
//   0.238. If the leaning tail had NOT settled it, the next lever would have been lightening the 16
//   numerals to 1.0 (0.19, inside Bootstrap's range), which would give a 1.5 counter on a 3.5 bowl
//   — but that is a rule change, numerals currently keep the outline stroke, and it is not needed.
//
// HOW THE LEAN IS BUILT, derived here and not transcribed. A straight tail leaving the bowl's 3
// o'clock re-enters the bowl, because the tangent there is vertical: a chord leaning t degrees off
// vertical from a point t degrees short of its own tangent runs back inside its own bowl. So the
// only clean lean is the TANGENT one — a tail leaning t degrees leaves the bowl exactly t degrees
// past 3 o'clock, where the bowl's own tangent is already that angle, and the two merge with no
// corner and no overlap. A 30 degree lean therefore leaves at 4 o'clock, which is inside the
// survey's 3 to 4:30. M, N and O leave LOWER than their tangent on purpose, which puts a small
// corner at the join; that is the one place a departure angle is a free variable.
//
// WHAT EACH CANDIDATE REPORTS. Lean off vertical; where it leaves the bowl, in degrees past 3
// o'clock; how much of the letter's ink projects below the bowl, as a percentage, against the
// field's 24-33; and where the terminal lands relative to the BOWL'S OWN CENTRE, positive meaning
// left of it — the number round 2 established as the thing to aim at. Bowl share, counter and ink
// box per master as before. Bowl and tail terminal arcs are circular with exact radii, so the
// D-slot ratio is 1.00 everywhere.
//
// THE GROUPS. 12 straight leans on the tangent (A-F a 67 per cent bowl from 24 to 40 degrees,
// G-J a 71 per cent bowl, K-L a 62 per cent bowl); 3 that leave below the tangent, for the corner
// at the join (M-O); 4 that run straight and then turn the terminal, the two constructions on one
// drawing (P, Q, R, U); 2 at Font Awesome's 76 per cent bowl, which round 1 said could not read as
// a 9 (S, T); and 4 CURVED reference points kept unchanged so the comparison is direct — V is round
// 2's recommendation, W is old J, X is round 2's straightened terminal and Y is old I.
//
// THE ANSWER TO THE QUESTION THAT WAS ASKED, in plain words: at 16 the lean reads better than the
// turn. A straight diagonal puts its ink on a new pixel at every step, so it rasterises as a
// staircase of separate dark pixels running away from the bowl and the eye reads a stroke going
// somewhere. A quarter turn spends its length changing direction, so the same amount of ink is
// spread over the same few pixels and rasterises as grey hugging the bowl. Compare D against V at
// 16 and it is not close. Round 2's finding still stands, though: the tail must reach the baseline,
// or the 9 floats against the 1 it pairs with.
//
// The filled twin draws the bowl as its own outlined ring (outer edge r + stroke/2, inner edge
// r - stroke/2, evenodd) and keeps the tail as a line at the outline weight, so a numeral holds the
// outline weight in both styles as list-ordered-filled does and the two ink boxes are identical.
// A lean of anything but 0 or 90 degrees puts its endpoints off the master's grid; those are
// rounded to the master's decimal places and are the documented exception in each file.
